<CaseStudy title="Security Workflow Automation" description="Two n8n workflows connecting security alerts, enrichment, response decisions, and analyst notifications." category="Hands-on integration / Independent interview project" role="Workflow design, integration & demonstration" scope="n8n · VirusTotal · Okta · Slack" next="/work/riker-bot/" nextLabel="Riker Bot">
  <p slot="notice" class="case-notice">Built in n8n for a Torq interview using a trial sandbox and simulated alerts. This independent demonstration combines API integrations with explicitly simulated response steps.</p>
  <Fragment slot="links"><a class="button" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fR2BJcEP8eI">Watch the full demo on YouTube ↗</a><span class="download-detail">9 minutes 37 seconds</span></Fragment>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem</p><h2>Give an alert enough context<br />to decide what happens next.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Security teams need to turn incoming alerts into a useful next action. That often means collecting context from several services, deciding whether intervention is warranted, and giving an analyst a clear summary.</p><p>I built two workflows to demonstrate that process: phishing triage and identity threat response. My contribution covered intake, field normalization, API integrations, conditional branches, and the outputs used to explain the result.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / Phishing triage</p><h2>Enrich the submission.<br />Keep review paths explicit.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>A webhook accepts a simulated phishing submission. The workflow normalizes its fields, checks for a URL, submits it to VirusTotal, and retrieves the analysis result.</p><p>Conditional branches use the available reputation results and input severity to choose a response. Missing URLs or unavailable enrichment route to manual review. A structured summary carries the result into Slack.</p><p>The prototype models ticket creation and quarantine through output fields. Those steps illustrate the intended downstream handoff; they do not call a ticketing system or quarantine an actual email.</p></div></section>
  <figure class="deck-figure"><a href="/images/phishing-triage-workflow.webp" aria-label="Open phishing triage workflow screenshot at full size"><img src="/images/phishing-triage-workflow.webp" width="1050" height="340" loading="lazy" alt="n8n phishing workflow from webhook intake through VirusTotal analysis, manual review branches, simulated ticket fields, and Slack notification" /></a><figcaption><span>Phishing triage · Screenshot from the sandbox demonstration</span><a href="/images/phishing-triage-workflow.webp">Open full-size screenshot ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Identity threat response</p><h2>Connect the response<br />to a visible outcome.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>A simulated suspicious-login alert supplies the user, IP address, country, and risk score. The workflow normalizes those fields and adds GeoIP context before branching on the supplied risk score.</p><p>The high-risk path looks up the user in an Okta trial tenant and calls the session-revocation API. It checks the response status and routes to successful-containment or failed-containment handling. The low-risk branch records that automated containment is not required.</p><p>The workflow assembles incident fields and a Slack notification with the alert context, containment result, and escalation guidance. Incident creation is modeled locally rather than written into an external case-management platform.</p></div></section>
  <figure class="deck-figure"><a href="/images/identity-response-workflow.webp" aria-label="Open identity response workflow screenshot at full size"><img src="/images/identity-response-workflow.webp" width="1050" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="n8n identity workflow with GeoIP enrichment, risk assessment, Okta user lookup and session revocation, success and failure branches, and SOC notification" /></a><figcaption><span>Identity response · High-risk, low-risk, and containment-failure paths</span><a href="/images/identity-response-workflow.webp">Open full-size screenshot ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">04 / The analyst’s view</p><h2>Make the result<br />easy to act on.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The sandbox notification brings the user, source IP, location enrichment, risk score, and reported containment outcome into one message. That gives the analyst context beyond a simple workflow-success indicator.</p><p>The screenshot below shows the demonstrated identity-response result. It is evidence of the sandbox workflow’s output, not a claim about production incidents or measured response-time savings.</p></div></section>
  <figure class="deck-figure"><a href="/images/identity-slack-result.webp" aria-label="Open sandbox Slack result screenshot at full size"><img src="/images/identity-slack-result.webp" width="910" height="241" loading="lazy" alt="Sandbox Slack notification showing a high-risk login, GeoIP context, risk score, and reported successful Okta session revocation" /></a><figcaption><span>Slack notification · Actual output shown in the demo</span><a href="/images/identity-slack-result.webp">Open full-size screenshot ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">05 / Design decisions</p><h2>Prove the integration.<br />Explain its boundaries.</h2></div><div class="prose"><h3>Normalize before branching</h3><p>Consistent fields make the alert context available across enrichment, decision, and notification steps.</p><h3>Keep uncertainty visible</h3><p>A missing URL or unavailable reputation result has a review path. A failed containment response has an escalation path. The workflow makes those decisions inspectable.</p><h3>Separate the prototype from deployment</h3><p>The exercise focuses on orchestration and explanation. Production work would require further validation of asynchronous analysis completion, retries, field propagation, decision thresholds, and operational controls.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-callout"><p class="eyebrow">See the walkthrough</p><h2>From incoming alert<br />to analyst notification.</h2><p>The original recording walks through both workflows, their decision branches, and the sandbox results.</p><a class="button secondary" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fR2BJcEP8eI">Watch the full demo on YouTube ↗</a></section>
</CaseStudy>
